1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,696
For five episodes, one phrase has
followed every number: rough estimates.

2
00:00:04,736 --> 00:00:12,638
The step is 2.02. Alder sits 0.87 above
Cedar. Rough, we said, and moved on.

3
00:00:12,767 --> 00:00:14,519
Today we pay that debt.

4
00:00:14,559 --> 00:00:19,146
The dial reads 2.02, which multiplies
the odds by seven and a half.

5
00:00:19,186 --> 00:00:24,786
Would you stake much on seven and a
half? On somewhere between five and ten?

6
00:00:24,933 --> 00:00:28,270
Hold your guess. The answer
is in the shape of the hill.

7
00:00:29,867 --> 00:00:32,088
Welcome back to Model in Motion.

8
00:00:32,128 --> 00:00:34,057
Today: how sure are we?

9
00:00:36,133 --> 00:00:40,850
Here is the hill from Episode 6: the
height for each position of the dial.

10
00:00:40,890 --> 00:00:46,808
Its top is at 2.02. The question is how
far from the top the truth could be.

11
00:00:46,933 --> 00:00:49,867
That depends on how sharply the top is curved.

12
00:00:49,907 --> 00:00:53,677
A sharp peak pins the dial.
A flat one lets it wander.

13
00:00:53,717 --> 00:00:59,827
And we already have the curvature. It is
the folded number from last time: 2.93.

14
00:00:59,967 --> 00:01:04,578
One over its square root gives
the standard error: 0.58.

15
00:01:04,618 --> 00:01:08,908
Go about two of those either side of the top,

16
00:01:08,948 --> 00:01:13,821
and you have a 95%
interval: from 0.87 to 3.16.

17
00:01:13,967 --> 00:01:19,794
As a multiplier on the odds, that
is anywhere from 2.4 to 23.6.

18
00:01:19,834 --> 00:01:23,324
So the data are clear that
higher risk raises the odds.

19
00:01:23,364 --> 00:01:27,309
They cannot say whether by a
factor of two or of twenty.

20
00:01:28,900 --> 00:01:32,700
Could the true step be zero?
The hill answers in three ways.

21
00:01:32,740 --> 00:01:37,137
Across: zero lies about three and a
half standard errors from the top.

22
00:01:37,177 --> 00:01:41,319
Down: the hill at zero
stands 7.3 below the top.

23
00:01:41,359 --> 00:01:45,591
And by the slope: at zero, the
ground is still climbing steeply.

24
00:01:45,733 --> 00:01:48,136
Three different measurements, one verdict.

25
00:01:48,176 --> 00:01:52,654
A step this large would turn up by
chance less than once in a thousand.

26
00:01:54,233 --> 00:01:57,375
Now the clinics. Each one
has a little hill of its own,

27
00:01:57,415 --> 00:02:00,124
and its curvature is that clinic's wobble.

28
00:02:00,164 --> 00:02:10,604
Alder's is 6.38, so on its own its standard
error is 0.40. Dune's is 2.31: 0.66.

29
00:02:10,733 --> 00:02:16,645
Notice what earns precision. Not the number
of patients, but what each patient adds:

30
00:02:16,685 --> 00:02:20,720
most near 50%, almost nothing
near zero or a hundred.

31
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:24,193
Elm has 11 patients and a wobble of 0.02.

32
00:02:24,233 --> 00:02:29,750
A hundred patients at 2% would
carry less than Dune's twelve.

33
00:02:31,333 --> 00:02:33,902
But a clinic's gamma is not read on its own.

34
00:02:33,942 --> 00:02:38,845
It is read where the risk factor is zero,
and getting there leans on the dial.

35
00:02:38,885 --> 00:02:46,853
Birch's patients are mostly higher-risk,
so its gamma at zero borrows heavily:

36
00:02:46,893 --> 00:02:54,531
0.65, against 0.39 on its own. Alder
barely borrows: 0.41 against 0.40.

37
00:02:54,667 --> 00:02:59,235
And here zero bites again. Record
the risk factor three units higher,

38
00:02:59,275 --> 00:03:05,236
and Alder's standard error swells to 1.9,
though no gap between clinics has changed.

39
00:03:05,367 --> 00:03:10,212
So read each clinic where the patients
actually are: at the average case mix.

40
00:03:10,252 --> 00:03:15,904
There the standard errors are
0.43, 0.47, 0.52 and 0.66,

41
00:03:15,944 --> 00:03:24,426
however the risk factor is recorded. These
are the ones a fair comparison needs.

42
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,555
Put it in one picture. Each clinic's
gamma, with its 95% interval.

43
00:03:31,700 --> 00:03:34,113
Every interval overlaps every other.

44
00:03:34,153 --> 00:03:39,146
With 103 patients, the model has an
opinion about the order of these clinics,

45
00:03:39,186 --> 00:03:41,693
and very little confidence in it.

46
00:03:41,833 --> 00:03:47,169
And Elm. Its printed interval
runs from minus 19.7 to plus 7.2.

47
00:03:47,209 --> 00:03:51,484
That is not an estimate. It is a warning sign.

48
00:03:51,633 --> 00:03:55,783
One caution about the picture.
Each interval is about one clinic.

49
00:03:55,823 --> 00:04:00,879
Whether two clinics differ is a separate
calculation, and it comes later.

50
00:04:02,467 --> 00:04:05,435
So what would it take to
be sure? Ask the crowd.

51
00:04:05,475 --> 00:04:14,593
Among its 200 providers, those with 20 to
30 patients have a standard error near 0.55.

52
00:04:14,633 --> 00:04:19,350
Near 100 patients: 0.26. Above 400: 0.12.

53
00:04:19,467 --> 00:04:22,030
Four times the patients, half the error.

54
00:04:22,070 --> 00:04:27,149
For a standard error of 0.1
at a 20% readmission rate,

55
00:04:27,189 --> 00:04:30,953
a provider needs about 625 patients.

56
00:04:32,533 --> 00:04:34,029
Let's gather it up.

57
00:04:34,069 --> 00:04:39,448
A sharp peak means a small standard error:
one over the square root of the curvature.

58
00:04:39,488 --> 00:04:43,288
A clinic's uncertainty is its
own, plus a share of the dial's.

59
00:04:43,328 --> 00:04:47,795
And for a fair comparison, read
every clinic at the average case mix.

60
00:04:47,933 --> 00:04:51,434
All of it assumes patients are
independent of one another.

61
00:04:51,474 --> 00:04:55,792
Count the same patient twice, and
these intervals are too narrow.

62
00:04:55,933 --> 00:05:00,742
We have left one clinic waiting
since Episode 4. Next time: Elm.

63
00:05:00,867 --> 00:05:04,972
This has been Model in Motion.
Thanks for following the story.
